                <pb facs="0902" n="788" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg788"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">550.E1/247: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Child</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Genoa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-05-06T16:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 6, 1922—4
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 6—2:10 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>31. Your unnumbered telegram of May 4, 7 p.m. Krassin wishes to meet me, but I
                    propose that I do not see him until Russia has replied to the latest memorandum
                    from the Allies, and it may be not at all. Unless I receive instructions, I will
                    continue to be guided by my own discretion. Present status seems to me
                    satisfactory and I consider American interests to be less endangered than was
                    indicated by somewhat hysterical Paris reports. In answer to informal inquiry
                    from the French delegates I have said that I could not discourage any proposal
                    to have them here openly condemn all secret negotiations or bargains with Soviet
                    Russia or with others, either while the Conference is in session or immediately
                    after it adjourns.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Child</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
